SJC 13721 . Town of Concord versus Neil Rasmussen and others . Okay , Attorney King , whenever you 're ready . All set . Good morning . May it please the Court . Gwen King , on behalf of the appellants . Before the Court today is a question of both how a way becomes public and also how a public way is discontinued and ceases to be public . During my time , I 'd like to address three issues for the Court . First , the effect of the 1932 discontinuance by the Middlesex County Commissioners under the 1924 act to discontinue public ways . Second , whether a way can be deemed public by virtue of a lost layout where there is no evidence regarding when it was laid out , who it was laid out by , and most importantly , under what authority it was laid out . And third , whether the Court should eliminate the high bar of establishing a public way by prescription , where the claim of public prescription is an ancient claim . First , as to the effect of the 1932 discontinuance , a fundamental rule of land law is that a way is public until it is -- until it is legally discontinued . Well , what is a legally -- a legal discontinuance ? It is a discontinuance according to law . In 1924 , the legislature — Just to be more specific . There are three different ways , right ? There 's 32A . There 's 21 and 30 . Right ? Correct . Are n't you sort of saying all three work exactly the same ? I 'm -- that 's -- I 'm a little confused by your argument that they 're not doing different things . Well , they definitely have different procedures and in some instances different outcomes . So section 21 , let 's take that first . Just just pin you down and I 'll let you talk . But my understanding is that your view all three of those turn it into a purely private way . Right ? Well I -- with a slight distinction , Your Honor . So under Nylander , this Court held a section 21 discontinuance , which is a discontinuance at town meeting of a town way . And only a town way under section 21 does n't leave any abutter access rights . There is no private way left intact after section 21 discontinuance per this court in Nylander . However — I thought the footnote in Nylander is the big , foundation -- is the foundation of their argument for why this remains still public . So the footnote that 's emphasized by the town is referring to a subsequent version of 32A . And in that this court noted that that leaves intact public access rights . Now , we have contended in our brief that what the court was construing is public access rights is distinct from how the town characterized it . Most notably , as you can see in the underlying appeals court and superior court decision in Nylander public access , private way was referring to abutters ' private rights of access . And I emphasized that as an important point because , as we note , the public right to travel is the public easement that is laid out under chapter 82 . The public right of access is is not something in this court . In the 2002 case , Chandler actually noted the distinction and said , you ca n't lay out a way for access . You can onl- -- the public right is only that of travel , and that 's inconsistent with what is clearly the legislature 's intent in the 1924 act , because in the 1924 act , they say it 's no longer needed for common convenience and necessity , and it 's not needed to be safe and convenient for travel . That 's inconsistent with the concept of what a layout for a public way is needed to be for , to be safe and convenient for travel . The inverse decision was adjudicated by the county commissioners here , and the adjudication is a very important point as to the distinct meaning of section 32A . Can you ? I have , I think , a somewhat related question to Justice Kafka 's question . If we look at section 21 , as it was in effect in 1932 , and section 32A as it was in effect at that time , uh , what is the difference in outcome ? Are you saying that it -- that action under either section would result in exactly the same outcome ? No , I 'm not . And the question actually shows why section 32A was so important to municipalities and the Commonwealth at the time . So first , as to the jurisdiction of section 21 , that only applies to town ways . However , the jurisdiction of 32A applies to public ways which the county commissioners had jurisdiction over all public ways at the time . And it 's important to go back in time to 1924 when thinking about the jurisdiction of the county commissioners . They had authority over public ways being county highways , town ways , and any public way of unknown jurisdiction . So that 's an important distinction because here where , as made clear by the the case before you , what this way was was at minimum uncertain . So the county commissioners would have the authority to discontinue it under 32A , whereas the town would not have authority to discontinue a county way under section 21 . I did n't see anything to suggest this was a county way . Well , to the contrary , the town contends that there was a 1763 county layout of the Northern Trail that was effectuated by the county commissioners . We have disputed the -- whether that was effective because the conditions -- precedents -- precedents were not fulfilled . The town notes in their brief that -- and relies and emphasizes that certain deeds of the Northern Trail said -- referred to the way as a county way .